WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:04.080
Comic book stories are no longer just Marvel
and DC's domain.

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:08.640
Writer Mark Millar's series "Jupiter's Legacy"
got the Netflix treatment in 2021 with an

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:09.640
8-episode season.

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:13.559
After years of work spent bringing it to life,
it was cancelled after only one month.

00:00:13.559 --> 00:00:15.250
Here's the untold truth of the short-lived
show.

00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:18.590
When Stan Lee gives you comic advice, you
take it.

00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:22.700
Lee co-created Marvel's most iconic characters,
but he never owned his own Marvel work — which

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:26.530
led to a much lower cut of the profits from
Lee's characters than he deserved.

00:00:26.530 --> 00:00:30.910
Lee even sued Marvel in 2002, dismayed with
the meager cut he received from the first

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:31.960
"Spider-Man" movie.

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:35.660
Mark Millar worked for Marvel when Lee encouraged
him to break from the company to create his

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:36.660
own content.

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:40.020
Wanting to help Millar avoid the same mistakes
he made, Lee coached the budding comic book

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:42.600
writer to write for himself instead of a large
company.

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:46.890
Speaking with Business Insider in 2015, Millar
revealed some of the helpful advice that Lee

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:47.890
had given him:

00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:51.340
"He said, 'Financially, now it's so much better
for you guys than it ever was for me.

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:53.500
You could own the rights to your own movies
and books.

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:55.370
You could license this stuff to people, like
toys.

00:00:55.370 --> 00:00:58.750
If I had the opportunity you've had, I'd have
killed for it.'"

00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:00.610
"And with great power, comes—"

00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:01.860
"Great responsibility."

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:03.300
"No.

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:06.180
Well, yeah."

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:09.850
Given the success that Millar has had as a
creator in comics, movies, and TV, it seems

00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:12.040
like Stan Lee's advice was worth listening
to.

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:16.420
It's not often that a major studio buys an
entire company, but that's precisely what

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:21.200
happened with Mark Millar's Millarworld company
in 2017 when Netflix bought the whole shebang

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:22.380
for around $40 million.

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:26.950
Of course, the acquisition includes all of
Millar's comic works from the company beyond

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:30.280
"Jupiter's Legacy" — including properties
like "Kick-Ass," "Kingsman," and "The Magic

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:34.620
Order," Netflix's first comic book series,
which was released after the company purchased

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:35.620
Millarworld.

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:38.981
Given the prior success of Millar's many works
on screen, the purchase doesn't seem to have

00:01:38.981 --> 00:01:41.119
been a huge risk for the streaming giant.

00:01:41.119 --> 00:01:45.220
Given that the acquisition happened in 2017,
what took so long for the series to end up

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:46.280
on Netflix?

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:50.140
Millar told The Hollywood Reporter in April
2021 that he had spent time focusing more

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:54.170
on the series' quality, rather than worrying
about how quickly he could get it out, saying,

00:01:54.170 --> 00:01:56.570
"You can do it fast, or you can do it brilliant.

00:01:56.570 --> 00:01:57.580
That's my philosophy."

00:01:57.580 --> 00:02:01.720
Still heeding Stan Lee's advice, Millar made
sure that he would remain president of Millarworld

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:02.720
even after the acquisition.

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:06.500
In a May 2021 press release about "Jupiter's
Legacy," Millar noted,

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:11.239
"When my wife, Lucy, and I sold Millarworld
to Netflix in 2017, our first priority was

00:02:11.239 --> 00:02:12.329
'Jupiter's Legacy.'

00:02:12.329 --> 00:02:15.840
We had flirted with the idea of a movie, but
Netflix afforded us the chance to make this

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:18.180
adaptation as big and deep as it needed to
be."

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:23.019
Millar first conceptualized the "Jupiter's
Legacy" series in 2008, but the first issue

00:02:23.019 --> 00:02:25.409
wasn't published until 2013.

00:02:25.409 --> 00:02:29.239
Millar handled the words while Frank Quitely
handed the series' illustrations, and Pete

00:02:29.239 --> 00:02:30.750
Doherty took on the lettering.

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:34.519
As Millar told The Hollywood Reporter, the
"Jupiter's Legacy" comics are the longest-running

00:02:34.519 --> 00:02:37.799
series Millar has ever published, spanning
over 20 issues.

00:02:37.799 --> 00:02:41.689
The Netflix series covers five issues over
a single eight-episode season.

00:02:41.689 --> 00:02:45.529
Compared to comic book universes like DC and
Marvel, eight years between publishing the

00:02:45.529 --> 00:02:49.829
first comic and landing a massive Netflix
deal for the series isn't exactly a long wait

00:02:49.829 --> 00:02:50.829
time.

00:02:50.829 --> 00:02:54.459
Some comics take decades to get adapted for
the screen, so going from the page to a full-scale

00:02:54.459 --> 00:02:56.909
film production in that short a time is far
from ordinary.

00:02:56.909 --> 00:03:01.299
Of course, it helps that "Jupiter's Legacy"
is far from Mark Millar's first foray into

00:03:01.299 --> 00:03:02.299
Hollywood.

00:03:02.299 --> 00:03:05.650
With his resume, it was only a matter of time
before "Jupiter's Legacy" was added to his

00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:06.650
filmography.

00:03:06.650 --> 00:03:10.620
Few directors have had more success with superhero
movies than James Gunn.

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:14.449
The director brought "Guardians of the Galaxy"
to the big screen for the first time, establishing

00:03:14.449 --> 00:03:16.890
a new beloved franchise in the Marvel film
canon.

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:21.459
He also picked up the pieces from DC's "Suicide
Squad" property with a reimagined follow-up,

00:03:21.459 --> 00:03:23.459
2021's "The Suicide Squad."

00:03:23.459 --> 00:03:27.620
So when Gunn gave Millar advice about what
he should do with his "Jupiter's Legacy" property,

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:29.069
Millar had good reason to listen.

00:03:29.069 --> 00:03:33.870
While "Jupiter's Legacy" was initially conceived
as a trilogy film series in 2015 with Lorenzo

00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:38.439
di Bonaventura in a production role, Millar
discarded the plan with a push from Gunn.

00:03:38.439 --> 00:03:41.829
During an interview with Den of Geek, Mark
Millar revealed that Gunn told him that the

00:03:41.829 --> 00:03:43.430
comics "could never work as a movie."

00:03:43.430 --> 00:03:46.939
Much of Millar's success comes from listening
to those who have come before him, and he

00:03:46.939 --> 00:03:49.340
proved more than willing to listen to this
advice.

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:53.239
As Millar told Den of Geek, quote, "the smartest
guy in the world is James Gunn."

00:03:54.239 --> 00:03:57.720
Most TV shows with heavy backstory take an
episode or two to lay out the origin story

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:02.159
when delving into the characters' histories
— but Mark Millar had other ideas with "Jupiter's

00:04:02.159 --> 00:04:03.159
Legacy."

00:04:03.159 --> 00:04:06.029
Instead of spending a minute (or 40) delving
into the history of the first superheroes

00:04:06.029 --> 00:04:09.779
in the "Jupiter's Legacy" canon, we get to
watch the historical origins of the Union

00:04:09.779 --> 00:04:13.260
of Justice members be revealed throughout
the series, one piece at a time.

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:17.130
Jumping between past and present, the series
dips into its characters' Great Depression

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:20.570
backstory, covering their respective histories
when it's relevant to do so.

00:04:20.570 --> 00:04:23.470
So, what exactly influenced Millar to take
this route?

00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:27.010
During an interview with The Wrap, he revealed
that "The Godfather Part II" provided the

00:04:27.010 --> 00:04:29.410
inspiration for the series' woven time jumps.

00:04:29.410 --> 00:04:32.980
According to Millar, the idea was pitched
by Steven DeKnight, the original showrunner

00:04:32.980 --> 00:04:34.160
for "Jupiter's Legacy."

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:37.400
In the same way that Michael Corleone and
his father Vito's stories play out at the

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:42.240
same time, decades apart, so too do the stories
of Sheldon and Brandon in "Jupiter's Legacy."

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:43.240
Millar explained,

00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:46.330
"The 'Godfather II' structure shows the father
and the son at the same age.

00:04:46.330 --> 00:04:49.490
And that's the same thing that's been done
here, to show the young characters and the

00:04:49.490 --> 00:04:52.830
old characters at the same age and essentially
making the same mistakes."

00:04:53.900 --> 00:04:56.360
"Jupiter's Legacy" has an infinite number
of comic book parallels.

00:04:56.360 --> 00:05:00.800
However, the deepest connection that Skyfox
actor Matt Lanter sees might not be what you'd

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:01.800
expect.

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:04.930
The actor noted the similarities between his
"Jupiter's Legacy" character and his role

00:05:04.930 --> 00:05:09.500
as Anakin Skywalker on "Star Wars: The Clone
Wars" — and the outdated rules of conduct

00:05:09.500 --> 00:05:11.450
that plague the heroes of both worlds.

00:05:11.450 --> 00:05:15.260
Lanter chatted with Looper during an exclusive
interview, where he compared the rigid nature

00:05:15.260 --> 00:05:18.130
of the Jedi Council to the strict rules of
the Union in "Jupiter's Legacy."

00:05:18.130 --> 00:05:19.130
He said,

00:05:19.130 --> 00:05:23.190
"The Jedi Council seems antiquated and just
doesn't apply in every situation anymore.

00:05:23.190 --> 00:05:25.620
And the Jedi Council is so black and white
[in its thinking].

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:28.080
I think Anakin realized that things aren't
black and white.

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:32.180
In fact, there are shades of gray, and that's
exactly what's going on in 'Jupiter's Legacy.'"

00:05:32.180 --> 00:05:36.520
In both cases, these unwavering rules lead
to the downfall of an otherwise relatively

00:05:36.520 --> 00:05:37.520
moral hero.

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:42.240
The fickle beast that is Mother Nature something
that almost every series or movie deals with

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:43.720
at some point during shooting.

00:05:43.720 --> 00:05:47.880
When the weather decides to do something unexpected,
production has two options: Roll with it,

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:49.050
or halt production.

00:05:49.050 --> 00:05:52.870
Luckily for "Jupiter's Legacy," when the skies
opened for a dramatic snowstorm, the weather

00:05:52.870 --> 00:05:55.620
only enhanced the scene's otherworldly quality.

00:05:55.620 --> 00:05:59.520
During an exclusive interview with Looper,
cinematographer Nicole Hirsch Whittaker — director

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:03.560
of photography for several episodes of "Jupiter's
Legacy" — revealed the perils of filming

00:06:03.560 --> 00:06:05.360
in an unpredictable Canadian climate.

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:09.430
In one episode in particular, the series'
seventh, the weather wasn't cooperating, leading

00:06:09.430 --> 00:06:11.560
to heavy — and unscripted — snowfall.

00:06:11.560 --> 00:06:15.020
While they were shooting a scene in the woods
over the course of a few days, Whittaker explained,

00:06:15.020 --> 00:06:18.440
"We all went back to our hotels and woke up
in the morning, and there was two feet of

00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:20.070
snow on the ground, which is very Canadian.

00:06:20.070 --> 00:06:22.430
We were like, 'Oh my gosh, there's a snowstorm.'

00:06:22.430 --> 00:06:26.620
So basically we got to set, everybody talked
about it and decided this is an island that's

00:06:26.620 --> 00:06:28.200
alive, so let's embrace it.

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:31.140
[...] So they started writing, and within
an hour we had a new script."

00:06:31.140 --> 00:06:35.540
Luckily for fans, the surprise snow lends
an incredibly realistic backdrop to the scene.

00:06:35.540 --> 00:06:38.540
Given that the characters head to the island
to claim their superpowers, they deal with

00:06:38.540 --> 00:06:41.990
a whole host of challenges — now including
unpredictable weather.

00:06:42.990 --> 00:06:46.451
The first flashback we get in "Jupiter's Legacy"
kicks off the events of The Great Depression

00:06:46.451 --> 00:06:47.680
as the stock market crashes.

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:51.870
While it's undoubtedly a fascinating point
in American history, it's a unique time period

00:06:51.870 --> 00:06:55.310
to place the origin story of a group of slowly
aging superheroes.

00:06:55.310 --> 00:06:59.540
However, the period makes sense given the
year in which Millar began writing the comics.

00:06:59.540 --> 00:07:04.340
2008 marked a steady economic decline, leading
to a financial crisis of the likes the world

00:07:04.340 --> 00:07:06.130
hadn't seen since the Great Depression.

00:07:06.130 --> 00:07:10.440
"'The Death of Capitalism' — that is one
hell of a headline."

00:07:10.440 --> 00:07:12.620
Millar told The Hollywood Reporter in April
2021,

00:07:12.620 --> 00:07:16.281
"When I sat down to do this — it was three
years before I actually [wrote] it, during

00:07:16.281 --> 00:07:20.220
the financial crash in 2008 — I said, 'I'm
not going to do this unless I can make it

00:07:20.220 --> 00:07:22.310
the greatest superhero story of all time.'"

00:07:22.310 --> 00:07:26.280
Setting the superheroes' origin stories in
the late '20s with Great Depression ties certainly

00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:28.750
set the story apart from many of its counterparts.

00:07:28.750 --> 00:07:32.750
The choice roots the series in a reality that
almost everyone can relate to, and offers

00:07:32.750 --> 00:07:34.950
a unique vantage point for the story to cover.

00:07:35.950 --> 00:07:39.740
As we've seen with the Marvel Cinematic Universe
and DC Extended Universe, there's no shortage

00:07:39.740 --> 00:07:44.580
of stories waiting to be told when film studios
decide to tap into comics characters' legacies.

00:07:44.580 --> 00:07:48.150
With plenty of source material waiting to
be mined, "Jupiter's Legacy" seemed poised

00:07:48.150 --> 00:07:52.520
to enjoy a multiple-season run on Netflix
— but any hopes fans may have had for follow-up

00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:56.800
installments were dashed in early June of
2021, when Millar announced that the cast

00:07:56.800 --> 00:07:58.770
had been released from its contracts.

00:07:58.770 --> 00:08:02.510
Although Millar indicated that he hadn't quite
given up hope for more "Jupiter's Legacy"

00:08:02.510 --> 00:08:06.590
onscreen at some point, in the short term,
Netflix's decision means the show has been

00:08:06.590 --> 00:08:07.590
canceled.

00:08:07.590 --> 00:08:10.990
On the bright side, this frees Millar up for
other projects, like the upcoming "Supercrooks"

00:08:10.990 --> 00:08:12.870
series he's set to debut for the platform.

00:08:12.870 --> 00:08:16.790
It's still a bitter pill to swallow for viewers
who came to love the "Jupiter's Legacy" characters

00:08:16.790 --> 00:08:20.680
throughout the show, but they can always turn
to the comics for more stories — the latest

00:08:20.680 --> 00:08:24.760
chapter of the saga, "Jupiter's Legacy: Requiem,"
was already set to begin arriving in comic

00:08:24.760 --> 00:08:26.870
shops when the show's cancellation was announced.

00:08:26.870 --> 00:08:31.000
So, where exactly does Millarworld, and more
specifically, "Jupiter's Legacy," fall in

00:08:31.000 --> 00:08:32.000
the comic book landscape?

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:35.680
If you ask Matt Lanter, he'll tell you that
it's the future of comics — and deserves

00:08:35.680 --> 00:08:37.620
a spot at the top with DC and Marvel.

00:08:37.620 --> 00:08:40.870
In fact, we did ask Matt Lanter during a Looper
exclusive interview.

00:08:40.870 --> 00:08:41.870
He said,

00:08:41.870 --> 00:08:45.870
"I think five years from now, everyone's going
to know that it's Millarworld, it's DC, and

00:08:45.870 --> 00:08:46.870
it's Marvel.

00:08:46.870 --> 00:08:50.350
So get to know these characters now, be the
first on the train and have fun with it, man."

00:08:50.350 --> 00:08:52.889
That's a pretty big claim, but it's not without
merit.

00:08:52.889 --> 00:08:56.870
Even though the series isn't moving forward
on Netflix, the show still made a huge impression

00:08:56.870 --> 00:09:00.699
on viewers with a rock-solid debut on the
streamer, coming in as the number one program

00:09:00.699 --> 00:09:02.399
on the platform upon its release.

00:09:02.399 --> 00:09:07.269
Additionally, as far as box office is concerned,
Millar's 2010 film "Kick-Ass" brought in over

00:09:07.269 --> 00:09:13.319
$96 million worldwide, and "Kingsman: The
Secret Service" raked in over $414 million.

00:09:13.319 --> 00:09:17.620
Those are numbers that are in the range of
or even surpass some early MCU projects like

00:09:17.620 --> 00:09:21.990
"Captain America: The First Avenger," which
brought in over $370 million.

00:09:21.990 --> 00:09:25.829
In other words, just because "Jupiter's Legacy"
may be over on Netflix, that doesn't mean

00:09:25.829 --> 00:09:28.430
it's not still the start of something big.

00:09:28.430 --> 00:09:30.829
